[FEN "r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/4pn2/3p4/PP1Q4/2P1P1P1/4qPBP/RN3RK1 w - - 1 14"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1b2rk1/pp3ppp/4pn2/3p4/PP1Q4/2P1P1P1/4qPBP/RN3RK1_w_-_-_1_14"]

{ Both queens stand in the middle, and his knight on b1 still has to come out. }
14. Nd2 { His knight comes out to d2, right under your queen on e2. } 14... e5 { The pawn attacks his queen on d4. Every safe square she has leaves the knight on d2 without a guard. [%cal Ge5d4] } (14... Rd8 { A useful rook move, but it gives him a move to cover the knight. } 15. Bf3 Qa6 16. e4 e5 { His bishop on f3 chased your queen off e2, so the pawn push wins nothing. He is a little better. }) (14... Qh5 { The queen leaves e2 on her own, and the knight on d2 is no longer attacked. } 15. Nf3 Qg4 16. Qxg4 Nxg4 { The queens come off, the knight got away, and he is a little better. }) (14... Qg4 { This steps off e2, and nothing attacks the knight on d2 any more. } 15. f4 Bd7 16. Nf3 Rfd8 { His knight reached f3 safely, and he is a little better. }) 15. Qxe5 { His queen takes the pawn on e5. From there she no longer guards the knight on d2. } 15... Qxd2 { A free knight on d2. Your pawn pulled his queen off her post: that is deflection. One pawn given, one knight taken. } (15... Re8 { Chasing his queen gives her time to come back. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. a5 Rac8 { His queen is back on d4 guarding the knight, and you are a pawn down. }) (15... Bg4 { The bishop does nothing to his queen, and she walks back to d4. } 16. Qd4 Bf5 17. h3 Rfd8 { The knight on d2 is guarded again, and you are a pawn down. He is better. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: deflection, do not take the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/qDdKT3lT"]
[FEN "1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1 b k - 6 21"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r2kb1r/3q1ppp/1B2pn2/8/8/5Q2/PP3PPP/3R1RK1_b_k_-_6_21"]

{ Both queens face each other across the board, and his king still sits on e8. }
21... Qb7 { He offers the queens off on b7. } 22. Rd8+ { Check on d8. Your bishop on b6 guards that square, so only his rook on b8 can take, and that rook was the only guard of his queen. [%cal Gb6d8] } (22. Qxb7 { Taking the queen at once lets his rook take back, so it is still one queen each. } 22... Rxb7 23. Bd4 Be7 24. a4 O-O { The queens are gone, and you stay a piece down for a pawn. He is winning. }) (22. Bd4 { The bishop centralises, but his queen takes yours on f3 first. } 22... Qxf3 23. gxf3 Nd5 24. Rfe1 Kd7 { The queens are off, and you are still a piece down for a pawn. }) 22... Rxd8 { His rook had to take, and it has left b8. } 23. Qxb7 { A queen for a rook. The check pulled his rook off b8, and the queen it guarded fell. That is deflection. } (23. Bxd8 { The bishop takes the rook, but his queen takes yours on f3. } 23... Qxf3 24. gxf3 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { The queens are off, and he has two extra pieces for a pawn. You are lost. }) (23. Qxf6 { A knight is far smaller than the queen standing on b7. } 23... gxf6 24. Bxd8 Kxd8 25. Rd1+ Kc7 { You gave queen, rook and bishop for a knight and a rook. You are lost. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the decoy, the lesson"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/1AYjl6wU"]
[FEN "r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1 b - - 0 17"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2k3r/ppbn1ppp/8/3N4/8/7P/PPP2PP1/R3R1K1_b_-_-_0_17"]

{ Next trick: the decoy. Force a piece onto a square you chose, where something is waiting for it. You are a piece down here. }
17... Re8 { His rook challenges yours on the e column. Take it with check, and his king has to land on e8. [%cal Ye1e8] } 18. Rxe8+ { Check. His king takes back, and it lands on e8, one knight jump from c7. [%csl Re8] } (18. Red1 { Keeping your rook lets him keep his king on d8, far from your knight. } 18... Re6 19. Rd2 Rc8 20. Rad1 Rd6 { His pieces are all guarded, and you are still a piece down. He is winning. }) (18. c4 { A quiet pawn move lets him trade rooks on his own terms. } 18... Rxe1+ 19. Rxe1 Bd6 20. b4 b6 { His king stayed on d8, the fork never happened, and you are still a piece down. }) 18... Kxe8 { His king has landed on e8. Your knight on d5 can check it from c7, taking the bishop there. [%cal Yd5c7] } 19. Nxc7+ { Check, and the bishop is gone. From c7 your knight also hits his rook on a8. Watch it collect. [%csl Ra8][%tail] } (19. Re1+ { A second check lets his king slip back to d8, where it guards c7. [%csl Rc7] } 19... Kd8 20. Re7 Rc8 21. Rxf7 Be5 { You won the f7 pawn, but you are still a piece down for it. He is winning. }) (19. Rd1 { A calm rook move gives his bishop time to leave c7. } 19... Bd8 20. c3 Nb6 21. Nxb6 axb6 { The knights are traded, and you are still a piece down. }) 19... Kd8 20. Nxa8 { The knight takes the rook on a8. The check dragged his king onto e8, where the fork was waiting: that is the decoy. You started a piece down, and now you are a rook up. } 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: decoy with a pawn"]
