[FEN "r7/2pk2p1/2b1p3/4PP1p/7P/3BK1P1/8/2R5 b - - 0 34"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r7/2pk2p1/2b1p3/4PP1p/7P/3BK1P1/8/2R5_b_-_-_0_34"]

{ Rooks, bishops and a few pawns each. It looks like a quiet ending. }
34... Ra3 { His rook swings to the third row. Can a decoy drag his king somewhere worse? } 35. fxe6+ { The pawn takes on e6 with check. His king has to leave d7, the only square from which it guarded the bishop on c6. } (35. Kf4 { A safe king move, and the ending stays level. } 35... Bd5 36. Be2 exf5 37. Bxh5 c6 { His king still guards the bishop, the trick is gone, and it is a draw. }) (35. Ke2 { Your king steps out of the way, but his king stays on d7, guarding the bishop. } 35... Bf3+ 36. Kd2 Bd5 37. f6 gxf6 { The pawns trade off, and the ending is a draw. }) 35... Kxe6 { His king takes the pawn on e6. Every other king move left the bishop unguarded too, so he grabs a pawn for it. } 36. Rxc6+ { Your rook takes the bishop with check. The pawn dragged his king onto e6, on the same row: that is the decoy. A bishop for a pawn. [%cal Gc6e6] } (36. Kf4 { Your king steps up, and his rook starts checking. } 36... Ra4+ 37. Rc4 Rxc4+ 38. Bxc4+ Ke7 { The rooks are traded, his bishop on c6 survives, and it is a draw. }) (36. Kd4 { Your king walks forward, and his bishop escapes to d5. } 36... Bd5 37. Rxc7 Ra4+ 38. Ke3 Ra3 { You won the c7 pawn instead of a bishop, and it is a draw. }) 1-0


