[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/p1p1B1pp/1b6/3Q4/2p4P/2k5/P2R2PK/2q5_b_-_-_14_41"]

{ Both kings stand in the open. You still have a rook on d2, and his queen waits on c1. }
41... Qxd2 { His queen grabs your rook on d2. Is she really safe there? } 42. Bb4+ { The bishop checks from b4. Taking it drags his king off c3, the one square from which it guards his queen on d2. [%cal Gd5d2] } (42. Qe5+ { A check of your own, but he blocks with the queen on d4 and keeps your rook. } 42... Qd4 43. Qe1+ Kc2 44. Bg5 Qd6+ { You are still a rook down, and he is winning. }) (42. Qf7 { Your queen steps away, and you stay a rook down. } 42... Bd4 43. Qf1 Be5+ 44. Kh1 Kb2 { His king is safe on b2, and a rook down you are lost. }) 42... Kxb4 { His king takes the bishop on b4. [%csl Rb4] } 43. Qxd2+ { Check, and his queen is yours. The bishop dragged his king to b4, away from his queen: that is the decoy. A queen for a bishop. } (43. Qf7 { Your queen wanders off, and his queen on d2 goes free. } 43... Qxa2 44. Qxg7 Qc2 45. Qe7+ c5 { You gave the bishop for nothing and are still a rook down. He is winning. }) (43. a3+ { A pawn check, and his king steps back to c3 to guard his queen again. } 43... Kc3 44. Qe5+ Kc2 45. a4 Bd4 { His queen stayed on d2, you are far behind in material, and you are lost. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: decoy, order matters"]
