[FEN "7r/5pk1/5qp1/1pN5/1PpPp3/4P1P1/4Q1K1/2B5 w - - 0 45"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/7r/5pk1/5qp1/1pN5/1PpPp3/4P1P1/4Q1K1/2B5_w_-_-_0_45"]

{ A queen and a rook for you. A queen, a knight and a bishop for him. }
45. Qf2 { He offers the queens off on f2. } 45... Rh2+ { The rook checks on h2, and his king takes it. [%csl Rh2] } (45... Qxf2+ { Trading first lets his king take back on f2, and the rook check wins nothing. } 46. Kxf2 Rh2+ 47. Ke1 c3 48. Kd1 { His king walked to d1, and the ending is about level. }) (45... Qf5 { The queen steps back, and the trade is off. } 46. Qxf5 gxf5 47. Bb2 Kg6 { The queens came off anyway, and the ending is level. }) 46. Kxh2 { His king takes your rook on h2. } 46... Qxf2+ { Check, and his queen is yours. The rook dragged his king to h2, too far from f2 to take back: that is the decoy. A queen for a rook. } (46... Qc6 { The queen steps away, and you are simply a rook down. } 47. Bb2 Qd5 48. Kg2 Kg8 { You gave a rook for nothing, and he is winning. }) (46... Qd8 { Retreating throws away the point, and the rook on h2 is gone for nothing. } 47. Nxe4 Qh8+ 48. Kg1 f5 { A rook down, with his king safe on g1. He is winning. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: interference, the lesson"]
