[FEN "1r4k1/p1p2ppp/2P1p3/3pP3/1B3P1b/P3q2P/2Q3P1/5R1K b - - 1 24"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1r4k1/p1p2ppp/2P1p3/3pP3/1B3P1b/P3q2P/2Q3P1/5R1K_b_-_-_1_24"]

{ Now interference: put a piece on the line between a defender and what it guards, and the defender goes blind. }
24... Bg3 { His bishop jumps to g3. His queen on e3 guards it along the third row. Put your rook on f3, in between them. [%cal Yf1f3,Re3g3] } 25. Rf3 { The rook stands on f3, between his queen and the bishop, and it attacks the queen. She has to move. [%cal Rf3e3] } (25. Bc5 { The bishop attacks his queen, but she offers a trade on b3 and the bishop on g3 stays guarded. } 25... Qb3 26. Qxb3 Rxb3 27. Rf3 Rb1+ { The queens are off, his rook checks on b1, and he is better. }) (25. Bd6 { Nothing blocks the third row, so his queen still guards g3. [%cal Re3g3] } 25... Qb6 26. Qc3 Qb5 27. Ra1 Bxf4 { His bishop took your f4 pawn, and he is better. }) 25... Qd4 { His queen runs to d4. She is off the third row, and nothing guards the bishop on g3 now. [%csl Rg3][%cal Yf3g3] } 26. Rxg3 { A whole bishop. Your rook blocked the line from his queen to g3, she had to leave, and the bishop fell. That is interference. } (26. Qc1 { Guarding your back row lets his bishop step away. } 26... Bh4 27. Kh2 h5 28. Qg1 Qxg1+ 29. Kxg1 { The queens are off, his bishop escaped to h4, and he is better. }) (26. Qc5 { Offering the queens lets his bishop escape afterwards. } 26... Qxc5 27. Bxc5 Rb1+ 28. Bg1 Bh4 { The bishop got away to h4, his rook is active on b1, and he is better. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: interference on the diagonal"]
[Result "1-0"]
