[FEN "r4rk1/pbpp1ppp/1p3n2/3Pn3/1q2PR2/4B1P1/PPP1N1BP/R2Q2K1 b - - 4 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pbpp1ppp/1p3n2/3Pn3/1q2PR2/4B1P1/PPP1N1BP/R2Q2K1_b_-_-_4_13"]

{ Pieces everywhere in the middle. Interference again: look for a defender with a long line to guard. }
13... Qxb2 { His queen takes the pawn on b2. From there she guards his knight on e5, all the way down the long diagonal. [%csl Rb2,Re5] } 14. Bd4 { Your bishop lands on d4, right on that diagonal, and attacks his queen. From b2 she can no longer see e5. [%cal Rd4b2] } (14. Rxf6 { Taking the knight on f6 costs your rook: his pawn takes back. } 14... gxf6 15. Nf4 Qa3 16. Bd4 Qe7 { A rook for a knight, and his queen got back to e7 to guard e5. The clean win is gone. [%cal Re7e5] }) (14. a3 { A pawn move that guards nothing, and his bishop joins in. } 14... Ba6 15. Bd4 Bxe2 16. Bxb2 Bxd1 17. Bxe5 Bxc2 { A flurry of captures, both queens gone, and the position is about level. }) 14... Qa3 { His queen runs to a3. What was she guarding before she left the diagonal? } 15. Bxe5 { A free knight. Your bishop on d4 cut the line from his queen to e5: that is interference. } (15. Rxf6 { The knight on f6 was guarded, and his pawn takes back. } 15... gxf6 16. Nf4 c5 17. dxc6 dxc6 { A rook for a knight, the free knight on e5 was left alone, and it is about level. }) (15. Rf5 { Another attacker on f6, but his d pawn is about to cover e5. [%csl Re5] } 15... d6 16. Rxf6 gxf6 17. Nf4 f5 { The d6 pawn guards e5 now, and the position is level. [%cal Rd6e5] }) 1-0


