[FEN "r4rk1/pq3ppp/1pp1p3/2b5/8/2PR1Q2/P4PPP/2BR2K1 b - - 0 18"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r4rk1/pq3ppp/1pp1p3/2b5/8/2PR1Q2/P4PPP/2BR2K1_b_-_-_0_18"]

{ You are two pawns down, but your rooks own the d column. His queen sits on b7. }
18... Be7 { His bishop drops back to e7. } 19. Rd7 { Your rook lands on d7, between his queen on b7 and his bishop on e7, and it attacks the queen. [%cal Rb7e7] } (19. Bh6 { The bishop eyes g7, but his queen still guards e7 and he answers calmly. [%cal Rb7e7] } 19... Qb8 20. Bf4 Qe8 21. Qg4 f5 { He is still two pawns up and a little better. }) (19. Qg3 { The queen looks active, but he has time to bring the bishop to f6. } 19... Bf6 20. Bg5 Bxg5 21. Qxg5 h6 { The bishops are off, and you are still two pawns down. }) 19... Qa6 { His queen runs to a6 and leaves the seventh row. } 20. Rxe7 { A free bishop. Your rook on d7 cut the row between his queen and e7: that is interference. You win back more than the two pawns. } (20. Bf4 { A calm move, and his bishop gets away. } 20... Rae8 21. h4 Qxa2 22. Be5 Qa5 { His rook on e8 guards the bishop, he took your a2 pawn, and he is better. }) (20. Qxc6 { One pawn instead of a whole bishop. } 20... Bf6 21. a3 Qa5 22. g3 Qxc3 { His bishop escaped, he took your c3 pawn back, and he is better. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the in between move, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/EgygDkDv"]
[FEN "1k1r1r2/pp4p1/6q1/2Qp4/5bP1/2P4p/PPN3NP/R4R1K w - - 0 30"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/1k1r1r2/pp4p1/6q1/2Qp4/5bP1/2P4p/PPN3NP/R4R1K_w_-_-_0_30"]

{ New trick: the in between move. Before you take back, look for a stronger move first, like a check he must answer. }
30. Nxf4 { His knight takes your bishop on f4. Before you take back, check with your queen on e4. [%cal Yg6e4] } 30... Qe4+ { Check. His king must answer it, and from e4 your queen also guards f4 and hits his knight on c2. [%cal Ge4f4,Ge4c2] } (30... Rxf4 { Taking back at once lets his rook take yours, and your attack runs dry. } 31. Rxf4 Qxc2 32. Qf2 Qg6 { You traded rook and bishop for his two knights, and he is winning. }) (30... Qxc2 { You grab the other knight, but the one on f4 stays and joins in. } 31. Qd4 Qxb2 32. Nxh3 a5 { His queen on d4 and knight on h3 take over, and he is winning. }) (30... Qxg4 { A pawn, while your bishop on f4 is still gone. } 31. Ne3 Qxf4 32. Rxf4 Rxf4 { You gave your queen for a rook and a knight. He is winning. }) 31. Kg1 { His king steps to g1. } 31... Rxf4 { Now you take on f4, and your queen on e4 guards the rook there. The check put her on e4 first: that was the in between move. [%cal Ge4f4] } (31... Qxc2 { The wrong knight: the one on f4 stays, and your queen has left e4. } 32. Qf2 Qxf2+ 33. Rxf2 d4 { The queens are off, his knight on f4 lives, and you are a piece down. }) (31... a5 { A slow pawn move, and his pieces get time. } 32. Rae1 Qxc2 33. Nxh3 Rxf1+ 34. Rxf1 { You are a piece and a pawn down, and his queen on c5 is loose around your king. He is winning. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: a check in between"]
[Result "1-0"]
