[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/uleXM0S5"]
[FEN "2kr2nr/ppp2ppp/3pb3/q3n3/7Q/PBNPB2P/1PP3P1/R4K1R b - - 2 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2kr2nr/ppp2ppp/3pb3/q3n3/7Q/PBNPB2P/1PP3P1/R4K1R_b_-_-_2_15"]

{ His knight on e5 eyes your pawn on d3, and your bishop on b3 looks at e6. }
15... Nxd3 { His knight takes your pawn on d3. Before you take back, is there an in between move? } 16. Bxe6+ { Check first: the bishop takes on e6. He has to answer the check, and his knight on d3 can wait. } (16. cxd3 { Taking back at once lets his bishop take yours on b3. } 16... Bxb3 17. Kg1 Re8 18. Qg4+ Be6 { You lost your bishop, and you are two pawns down. He is better. }) (16. Kg1 { Your king tucks away, and his knight grabs b2 as well. } 16... Nxb2 17. Bd4 Nh6 18. Rb1 Nf5 { He is three pawns up, and his knights are safe. He is better. }) 16... fxe6 { His pawn takes back. His other answers to the check lose even more. } 17. cxd3 { Now the knight too. Bishops traded evenly, then you took his knight: that is the in between move. You are a pawn up. } (17. Qe4 { This aims at the knight, and it jumps away to e5. } 17... Ne5 18. b4 Qa6+ 19. b5 Qa5 { His knight is safe on e5, and he is two pawns up. }) (17. b4 { A pawn move gives his knight time to escape. } 17... Qf5+ 18. Kg1 Ne5 19. Bxa7 Nf6 { His knight got away to e5. You are a pawn down, and he is better. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: take the other one first"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/2y1isTsq"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/pbp2ppp/1p1ppn2/3P4/5P2/2B2N2/PPP1Q1PP/2KR1B1R b kq - 2 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qk2r/pbp2ppp/1p1ppn2/3P4/5P2/2B2N2/PPP1Q1PP/2KR1B1R_b_kq_-_2_10"]

{ Bishop against bishop, knight against knight. His bishop on b7 eyes your pawn on d5. }
10... Bxd5 { His bishop takes the pawn on d5. } 11. Bxf6 { You take the knight on f6 first: it was guarding his bishop on d5. His e6 pawn guards it too, but your queen pins that pawn to his king. [%cal Ge2e8] } (11. Rxd5 { Taking back at once lets his knight take your rook. } 11... Nxd5 12. Bxg7 Rg8 13. Bh6 Nc6 { You are the exchange down, and he is better. }) (11. g4 { A pawn push while his bishop stays on d5 with your pawn in its pocket. } 11... O-O 12. b3 a5 13. Rg1 a4 { You are two pawns down for nothing, and he is better. }) 11... Qxf6 { His queen takes back on f6. } 12. Rxd5 { And now the bishop. Your queen still pins the e6 pawn, so nothing takes back. Knight first, bishop second: that is the in between move. [%cal Ge2e8][%tail] } (12. Qe3 { Guarding instead of taking gives him time to castle. } 12... O-O 13. h4 Nd7 14. h5 h6 { He castled, so the e6 pawn guards the bishop again. He is two pawns up and better. }) (12. Kb1 { A safe king move, and his bishop runs home. } 12... Bb7 13. Qc4 Nc6 14. Bd3 O-O-O { His bishop went back to b7, and he is two pawns up. }) 12... Qxf4+ 13. Kb1 { He took your f4 pawn with check, and your king is safe on b1. You have an extra bishop for three pawns, and his king is stuck in the middle. } 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: in between, grab the rook"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/xsH9QpS3"]
[FEN "2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R b - - 1 33"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/2k4r/b1p5/2P2pp1/1p6/4P1p1/2Bp2Pr/PP3PK1/2R4R_b_-_-_1_33"]

{ His bishop on a7 and his rooks on h3 and h8 all point at your king. }
33... Bxf2 { His bishop takes your pawn on f2. } 34. Rxh3 { You take the rook on h3 first. The bishop on f2 can wait: your king can take it next move. } (34. Kxf2 { Taking the bishop at once lets his rooks come off in his favour. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Ke3 f5 { You are the exchange and a pawn down, and he is winning. }) (34. Bd2 { Saving your bishop lets the rooks trade on h1. } 34... Rxh1 35. Rxh1 Rxh1 36. Kxh1 Bxg3 { All the rooks are gone, and he is two pawns up. He is winning. }) 34... gxh3+ { He takes back with the g pawn, with check. } 35. Kxf2 { And now the bishop. Rook first, bishop second: that is the in between move. A bishop up for a pawn. } (35. Kh2 { Your king dodges the check, and his bishop on f2 stays alive. } 35... Be3 36. Rd1 d2 37. Bxf6 Re8 { His bishop escaped and his pawn on d2 is close to queening. He is a little better. }) (35. Kf3 { Your king steps away, and his h pawn runs through. } 35... h2 36. Kxf2 h1=Q 37. Rxh1 Rxh1 { His pawn queened and took your rook with it. He is winning. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the trapped piece, the lesson"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/PZImD4rt"]
[FEN "r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R w KQkq - 0 13"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r2qk2r/1pp3p1/p1nbbn1p/3p1p2/3P4/PQN1PN1P/1PPB1P2/R3KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_13"]

{ Last trick: the trapped piece. Attack a piece that has no safe square left, and it is yours. }
13. Qxb7 { His queen takes the pawn on b7. Send your knight to a5 to attack her. [%cal Yc6a5] } 13... Na5 { The knight attacks his queen. Every square she can reach is covered, so she has no safe square left. [%csl Rb7] } (13... Ra7 { The rook chases her, but she slips back to b3 with your pawn. } 14. Qb3 O-O 15. Rg1 Qe7 { She escaped with the b7 pawn, and the position is about level. }) (13... Bd7 { Guarding the knight lets her walk home. } 14. Qb3 Be6 15. Qa4 Bd7 { She is safe on a4 with your pawn, and the position is level. }) (13... Ne7 { A quiet knight move, and she gets time to leave. } 14. Ne5 O-O 15. f4 Rb8 { She keeps the b7 pawn and can still run, and the position is level. }) 14. Qxa6 { She grabs one more pawn on a6 on her way down. } 14... Rxa6 { Your rook takes the queen. Now watch. [%tail] } (14... O-O { Castling lets her walk out to e2. } 15. Qe2 Nc4 16. Ne5 Nxe5 { She escaped, and you are only a pawn up. }) (14... Nc4 { This chases her again, and she escapes with check on b5. } 15. Qb5+ c6 16. Qb7 Nxd2 { You won his bishop on d2, but she is still loose. A little better, not a queen up. }) 15. Bxa6 Qa8 { Your queen goes to a8 and hits his bishop on a6. A queen for a rook and two pawns: she was trapped, so she was yours. [%cal Ga8a6] } 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: the rook in the corner"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/471AM7KL"]
[FEN "rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R w KQkq - 0 10"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rn1qk2r/ppp2ppp/5b2/3P4/4P3/5b1P/PPP1QPP1/RN2KB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_10"]

{ The trapped piece again. One of his pieces is boxed into its corner. }
10. Qxf3 { His queen takes your bishop on f3. } 10... Bxb2 { Your bishop takes the pawn on b2 and attacks his rook on a1. His own knight on b1 and pawn on a2 box it in. [%csl Ra1] } (10... O-O { Castling is sensible, but his rook gets time to escape. } 11. Nc3 Re8 12. Bc4 Qd6 { His knight left b1, the rook is free, and he is better. }) (10... Nd7 { This gives him time to free the rook. } 11. c3 O-O 12. Nd2 Rb8 { His knight left b1 and the rook can breathe. You are a piece down, and he is better. }) 11. Qb3 { His queen attacks your bishop on b2. } 11... Bxa1 { The rook in the corner is yours. It had no square to run to: that is a trapped piece. Your bishop on f3 went, and you took a rook and a pawn for it. } (11... Be5 { Saving your bishop lets him untrap the rook. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { His knight left b1, the rook is free, and the position is level. }) (11... Bf6 { The retreat saves the bishop, but the rook in the corner goes free. } 12. c3 O-O 13. Nd2 Nd7 { His knight on d2 freed the rook, and it is about level. }) (11... Qf6 { Guarding the bishop lets his queen take b7 and then your rook. } 12. Qxb7 O-O 13. Qxa8 Bxa1 { You won his rook, but he took yours and a pawn. He is better. }) 0-1


[Event "Intermediate tactics: trap the queen"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/kLwPDHWx"]
[FEN "r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R b kq - 5 19"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r3k2r/pp1n1pp1/4p3/3pPnPp/2pP1Q1P/2B2N2/qPPK1P2/3R3R_b_kq_-_5_19"]

{ His queen has wandered all the way to a2, far from his king. }
19... g6 { He plays a quiet pawn move. } 20. Ra1 { The rook attacks her on a2. Every square she can run to loses her anyway: she is trapped. } (20. Rb1 { Close, but from b1 the rook leaves a4 open, and she slips out. } 20... Qa4 21. Ra1 Qb5 22. Ng1 b6 { She got out to b5, and he is better. }) (20. Ng1 { A knight move, and she has time to run. } 20... a5 21. Ne2 Qa4 22. Ra1 Qb5 { She escaped to b5, and he is better. }) 20... Qxa1 { She takes the rook, the best of a bad lot. } 21. Rxa1 { You take her back. She had no safe square: that is the trapped piece. A queen for a rook. } (21. Ng1 { You forgot to take back, and she walks away. } 21... Qa4 22. Ne2 O-O 23. Ng3 Ng7 { She is loose again, you are a rook down, and he is winning. }) (21. Rc1 { The rook on c1 cannot take her on a1, and she escapes. } 21... Qa4 22. Qh2 O-O 23. Qh1 Qc6 { His queen got away, you are a rook down, and you are lost. }) 1-0


[Event "Intermediate tactics: take her last square"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/cB9DXF3Y/z0yyRVrk"]
[FEN "3r1rk1/p2nppbp/2p2np1/qpPp4/N2P1B2/PP1BP2P/5PP1/R2Q1RK1 b - - 0 15"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/3r1rk1/p2nppbp/2p2np1/qpPp4/N2P1B2/PP1BP2P/5PP1/R2Q1RK1_b_-_-_0_15"]

{ His queen on a5 sits a long way from home. }
15... bxa4 { His pawn takes your knight on a4. } 16. b4 { The pawn attacks his queen on a5. } (16. Rb1 { A rook move, and she has time to escape. } 16... axb3 17. Rxb3 e5 18. Bh2 Ne4 { You lost the knight on a4 for nothing, and he is winning. }) (16. Bg3 { A quiet bishop move, and she walks away. } 16... axb3 17. Qxb3 e5 18. Qb4 Qc7 { She escaped to c7, and you are a knight down. He is winning. }) 16... Qb5 { His queen runs to b5. } 17. Bxb5 { Your bishop takes the queen. Her last square was guarded, so she was trapped. [%cal Gd3b5][%tail] } (17. Bd6 { A fancy bishop move, and his pawn takes it. } 17... exd6 18. Bxb5 cxb5 19. Rc1 Rfe8 { You won the queen after all, but gave a knight and both bishops. He is a little better. }) (17. Bc7 { Attacking the rook lets her step to b7. } 17... Qb7 18. Bxd8 Rxd8 19. f4 Qc7 { You won a rook for your bishop, but the knight on a4 is gone and she escaped. He is a little better. }) 17... cxb5 18. Rc1 { He took back on b5. You gave a knight and a bishop for his queen, all because she had no square left. That is the trapped piece. } 1-0
